A single memory cell can store some data, usually 8 bits, aka a byte.&nbsp;
A memory cell can be read and that returns the last value written.&nbsp;
A memory cell can be written with a new value and the causes the prior value to be discarded and replaced by that new value.&nbsp;
A cell that holds a byte can store any value from 0 to 255 — it can store 256 different values.

There is no emptiness indicator, no last-written timestamp, no data type indicator: memory cells are effectively typeless (or simply typed) from a programming language perspective.&nbsp;
Interpretation of the value is up to the program, and can vary greatly.&nbsp;
A byte could be seen as an unsigned integer, a signed integer (-128..127), as an ascii character, as element of an enum, as a "character" in a Unicode string, as part of a multi-byte integer or floating point data type.

"Memory" (sometimes called main memory in older parlance) is an set of memory cells.&nbsp; Each memory cell has two properties:
* a fixed or constant (and also unique) unsigned integer that is its address, and
* a variable value usually the size of a byte — 8 bits. 
If the cell size is byte the system is called byte addressable.

The addresses are fixed (and in the hardware encoded in wirings and/or mappings), whereas the variable value is encoded as storage bits (each bit is some sort of self-sustaining feedback loop).

Each memory cell’s address is unique within a running program aka process.&nbsp;
Addresses are unsigned integers, they start at 0 and grow larger from there.

Each memory cell within the set can be read (given address, returns its variable byte value) and can be written (given address & new value).&nbsp;
Each read returns the last value written to that memory cell.&nbsp; Each write replaces the memory cell’s prior value with a new value.&nbsp;
(There’s no history of prior values at an address.)&nbsp;
That is the memory concept: for each cell individually, remembering the last value written. 

Memory is sometimes referred to as an array (of memory cells), because the array data structure is similar: elements of an array start at index zero and grow larger; individual elements of an array can be read and written.&nbsp;
However, I prefer to reserve the term "array" for a data structure in a program — a higher level concept than raw memory itself.&nbsp;
(An array can be stored anywhere in memory; multiple arrays can be stored in different parts of the same memory.&nbsp;
Further, memory doesn't have a base address, it simply has its first index at 0.&nbsp;
An array has a base address and first element at index 0.&nbsp; This distinction is significant when it comes to the actual dereference operation to access memory cells vs. array elements.)

Addresses are grouped into dense ranges, used for things like machine code, heap data, stack data.&nbsp; 
Since each memory cell has a unique address we can look at an address within a running program and find what range group it belongs to.

Within a range, cells’ memory addresses are contiguous.&nbsp; So, if address 10000 is within one range, then so is 10001, and 10002, 10003 also (as long as we are not past at the end of the range).

We use consecutive memory cells (cells whose addresses are consecutive) to store values that need more storage than a single byte, such as a 32-bit or 64-bit integer, or strings of varying lengths.

When we refer to a multi byte item, we do so with the one single address that is the lowest (in numerical value) among all the addresses in the multi byte item.&nbsp;
So, the 32-bit integer at 10000 (which occupies memory cells at 10000, 10001, 10002, 10003).

The more memory cells in the set of memory cells, the larger number of bits are needed to hold an address to any memory cell.&nbsp;
So, on a 16-bit machine we might have a total possibility of 2^16 = 65536 memory cells, which then requires 16 bits to hold an address.&nbsp;
In this example, the highest memory address would be 0xffff (the lowest is always zero).

A pointer is a variable that can be held in a processor register or in some memory cells.&nbsp; A pointer is a variable that refers to another variable.&nbsp;
It does so by holding the value that is the lowest address within (first byte of) the other variable.&nbsp;
These variables are use to share the addresses of other variables between caller and callee, for example, or for an algorithm to know what data it is currently working on.&nbsp;
Unlike memory cells, pointers are usually known to have a type: a pointer to what.&nbsp; A programming language should help the programmer keep track of types.&nbsp;
We can dereference a pointer to access the other object being referred to.&nbsp; We can also increment/decrement a pointer, and its value will change by the size of the item it refers to.&nbsp;
So, a pointer to an int (int *p), when incremented (p++) will point to the next successive int in memory.&nbsp; On a byte addressable machine, that increment by one (element) in C will increment the pointer value by 4 (bytes, because the next successive int from 10000 would be at 10004).

Many software algorithms use arrays and indexes.&nbsp; (Here using the term array in the data structure sense.)&nbsp;
A pointer can substitute for an index to refer to a particular element of an array.&nbsp;
For example, we can create a pointer to the 10th element of an array: `int a[20]; int *p=&a[10];`.
Many algorithms that operate in terms of arrays an index variables can be converted to operate with arrays and pointer variables.&nbsp;
Since the pointer contains both the notion of a memory address and an index, combined, this can be more efficient than indexing into an array.&nbsp;
(It can also be more confusing.)&nbsp;
Indexing into an array, (say done in a loop as with sorting or searching) as in a[i] requires repeated computation of the specific address being referenced (this is usually a shift (e.g. to multiply by 4) followed by addition, sometimes done together on some processors).&nbsp;
Pointers on the other hand have this repeated shift & addition precomputed so that repeated `*p` is likely more efficient than repeated `a[i]`.&nbsp;
When using indexing, we expect an index to change in value; similarly for pointers, we expect pointers to change in value.&nbsp;
We can increment an index; we can increment a pointer; with both we are advancing to refer to the next element (of the array).

Beyond that it is up to the programmer to instruct the processor to read and write various memory cells such that the program remembers and computes.

(As an aside memory cells are also referred to as locations; while running programs, proccesses).

In C we can use pointers to refer to a variable stored in memory (we refer to it by the lowest address it has, when it occupies multiple cells), and that variable can be a singleton or an element of an array.&nbsp;
In C it is easy to share a slice (a sub-portion) of an array by using a pointer and a length.&nbsp;
Since this is the same way that a whole array would be shared, algorithms and libraries that accept arrays also work on slices without having to know. 

The ability to substitute a slice when a whole array is expected, is not available in original versions of Java and C#, who consider a whole array to be a single object, that can be indexed.&nbsp;
But a slice would be a combination of whole array reference, starting index, and length.&nbsp; However, that cannot substitute when a whole array is expected by library functions.&nbsp;
So, some languages have added a more first-class notion of slices that overcome this issue.&nbsp;
(Other options are that libraries take a starting index and length so they can work with slices or whole array (starting index set to 0).)
In C the two notions of the base (whole) array reference and starting index are combined to form a single pointer to the beginning of the slice (to the first element of the slice).

